  "problem": "Use a visual fraction model to find the value of $3+\\frac{2}{7}$ as an improper fraction.",
  "worked_solution": "Writing $3+\\frac{2}{7}$ as a visual fraction model, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14128162/assets/9797ccbc74bee1628f6c056415b831ee315d1e5d5b6c01496abdcc7b62537b2b.png)\n\nThe shape on the right is split into $7$ equal parts. We can split the first, second, and third shapes into $7$ equal parts as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14128162/assets/3b8c180326206b70caf96caef6d560ac57621a61a5bd3fc85d9e3bb1e7dfebf8.png)\n\nThere are $7+7+7+2=23$ shaded parts in total. Therefore, their sum is $\\frac{23}{7}.$",
